---
// Per-store marketing pixels (Meta / Google / TikTok) — the STOREFRONT half of Pemasaran → Pelacakan.
// Bootstraps only the pixels the merchant enabled (store_tracking_channels, resolved by store_id) and
// fires the standard commerce funnel. Self-contained (does not touch the first-party Analytics.astro),
// but uses the same path + de-dupe rules so the two never disagree.
//
// Funnel → per-platform event:
//   PageView (every page) · ViewContent (/produk/<handle>) · InitiateCheckout (/checkout) ·
//   AddToCart (delegated click on #add-to-cart/#buy-now/[data-track=add_to_cart]) ·
//   Purchase (/checkout/sukses, once per order, with value + currency IDR + content_ids).
//
// IDS — the two rules that make any of this measurable (ADR 0012 §14.3, ADR 0013):
//  1. `content_ids` are the SAME strings the product feeds emit as <g:id>. This script NEVER derives
//     them: the id is rendered by the page (`data-catalog-id`) or issued by the order API, both from
//     the one shared helper. Guessing is worse than sending nothing — a value that is not a real
//     catalog item matches silently, forever.
//     A browsing shopper has not picked a variant, so a multi-variant product is reported at GROUP
//     level with `content_type: "product_group"` (Meta's own grouping) rather than a fabricated
//     variant id — and the TYPE travels with the id, including into the cart, because a group id
//     announced as a single product matches nothing. PURCHASE is exact: its ids come from the ORDER.
//  2. `event_id` on Purchase is issued by the server (`purchase:{orderNumber}`) and sent with BOTH the
//     browser event and the Conversions API event. Same id = one conversion, not two. If the server
//     did not return one (an older cached checkout), the browser event is still sent — under-reporting
//     is recoverable, double-counting a merchant's ROAS is not.
// Advanced matching (opt-in per channel): on Purchase, hash the order's email/phone (SHA-256) and pass
// them to the pixel. NOTHING here may throw into the page — a tracking failure must never break checkout.
import { storeTrackingPixels } from "../data/tracking";

const { store } = Astro.locals;
let pixels: Awaited<ReturnType<typeof storeTrackingPixels>> = [];
try {
  pixels = await storeTrackingPixels(store.id);
} catch {
  pixels = []; // a broken read must never break the storefront
}
const meta = pixels.find((p) => p.channel === "meta") ?? null;
const google = pixels.find((p) => p.channel === "google") ?? null;
const tiktok = pixels.find((p) => p.channel === "tiktok") ?? null;

/*
 * IMPLEMENTATION NOTES for the inline script below.
 *
 * They live HERE, in the frontmatter, because an `is:inline` script is shipped BYTE FOR BYTE to every
 * shopper on every page — Astro does not minify it, so a comment inside it is a comment the browser
 * downloads. 11.9 KB of them were. Keeping the reasoning (this repo pays for lost reasoning) while not
 * making shoppers pay for it means keeping it on this side of the fence.
 *
 * ⚠️ The proper fix is to stop needing `is:inline`: pass the config as a JSON data island and let Vite
 * bundle+minify the script, which also makes it cacheable across pages. That is a refactor of a
 * money-adjacent file and wants its own change.
 */

// ── NOTE-1 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Declared BEFORE the bootstrap: the loaders below are wrapped in it. They are vendor-standard
// snippets, but they all run inside one IIFE — an exception in the first would leave the other
// two un-bootstrapped and fire nothing at all, which is a silent, total tracking outage.

// ── NOTE-2 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ---- bootstrap the enabled pixels ----
// One `safe()` PER vendor. Wrapping all three in a single guard was the bug the comment described:
// a throw in the first loader skipped the other two and fired nothing at all.

// ── NOTE-3 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Google Ads is a SEPARATE destination with its own id, configured alongside GA4 rather than
// instead of it — a merchant running both is the normal case, and one field could only ever
// serve one of them. `AW-123456789` is the account; the `/label` half identifies the action and
// is only used on the conversion call below.

// ── NOTE-4 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ---- helpers ----
// Canonical Indonesian phone, the SAME shape the server hashes (`orders.ship_phone`, canonical
// `62…` since migration 0048). This reads the RAW value the shopper typed, so `0812…` AND the
// bare `812…` both have to become `62812…` — miss either and the browser hashes a different
// string from the server, the identities never match, and nothing anywhere reports it.

// ── NOTE-5 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This script lives in <head>; the elements it inspects (#variant-picker) live in <body>. Reading
// the DOM inline therefore ALWAYS found null — the multi-variant branch below was structurally
// unreachable. Everything that touches the DOM goes through here.

// ── NOTE-6 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Fire ONE funnel event across every enabled pixel.
// `data`: {value, currency, contentIds, numItems, contentType, eventId, orderId}.
// `eventId` is passed to Meta as `eventID` and to TikTok as `event_id` — the de-dup key the
// server-side Conversions API repeats. GA4 de-dupes on `transaction_id` instead.

// ── NOTE-7 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Per-ITEM quantity is only knowable when the event describes ONE item. `qty` is the event total
// (the sum of every line), so using it per item multiplied a 2-line order to 4 units. When there
// is more than one id, the field is omitted — an absent quantity is read as 1, a wrong one is
// read as truth.

// ── NOTE-8 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `value`/`currency` on ViewContent and AddToCart is what lets Meta optimise on revenue rather
// than on raw event counts; `num_items` is documented for InitiateCheckout specifically (and
// NOT for Purchase, which is why the server event omits it too — the two halves of one sale
// must describe it identically).

// ── NOTE-9 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ⚠️ `Purchase`, not `CompletePayment`. TikTok's current standard-event list does not contain
// `CompletePayment`, and their names are case-sensitive. It MUST match `capi.ts`'s server
// event exactly: TikTok de-duplicates on (event_source_id, event, event_id), so a name that
// differs between the two halves means every purchase is counted twice.

// ── NOTE-10 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TikTok documents `content_type` and `content_ids` as TOP-LEVEL properties (all three of
// content_type / content_ids / contents are required for Video Shopping Ads). Nesting the type
// inside `contents[]` alone left the required pair missing.

// ── NOTE-11 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `content_id` (singular) is TikTok's flat form and only means anything for a single item;
// `content_ids` is Meta's spelling and TikTok does not document it. `contents[]` above is the
// multi-item form and always travels.

// ── NOTE-12 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ⚠️ `event_id` is TikTok's THIRD argument, not a property of `properties`. Putting it inside
// the payload — which looks entirely reasonable — means TikTok never sees a de-dup key, so
// every purchase is counted twice: once by the pixel, once by the Events API.

// ── NOTE-13 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ⚠️ `item_id` must equal the Merchant Center offer id — Google has NO group concept, so a
// group id matches nothing. `value` is OMITTED rather than sent as 0: a recorded zero-value
// conversion is worse than an absent parameter, because it drags the reported average down.

// ── NOTE-14 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ── Google Ads ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GA4 events do NOT record a Google Ads conversion. Google documents it as its own call with
// a `send_to` destination, and dynamic remarketing wants a DIFFERENT item shape from GA4's:
// `id` (not `item_id`) plus `google_business_vertical`. Sending GA4's shape to an `AW-` tag was
// the reason Google Ads recorded nothing at all.

// ── NOTE-15 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TikTok's click id arrives ONCE, as a URL parameter on the landing page, and is gone by the time
// the shopper reaches checkout — so it is persisted here, on the first page they see. It is
// TikTok's strongest match key, the role `_fbc` plays for Meta, and we were dropping it.
// Stored for 28 days, which is TikTok's own attribution window; the cookie survives the visit.

// ── NOTE-16 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The catalog id for the product page we are on, and whether it is a variant GROUP. A shopper
// browsing has not chosen a variant, so a multi-variant product is reported as its group — the
// id /feed/google.xml emits as <g:item_group_id>. Inventing `handle-1` here would report a
// variant they never looked at.
// What the shopper is looking at, as precisely as the page knows it.
// 
// Prefer the BUY BUTTON: the variant picker keeps `data-catalog-id` on it in step with the
// selection, so it is the EXACT catalog item id (`1042-3`). The fallback is the price element,
// which every PDP renders — including a price-on-request product, which has no buy button at all.
// 
// Why exact matters: Meta documents that `product_group` must NOT be used with AddToCart or
// Purchase ("people buy specific products"), Google has no group concept at all so a group id
// matches no Merchant Center offer, and TikTok recommends the sku_id. Nothing is ever DERIVED
// here — a guessed id matches no catalog item and reports nothing, silently.

// ── NOTE-17 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AddToCart — delegated (same selectors Analytics.astro uses), so page scripts stay untouched.
// Registered HERE, not synchronously: a click that landed before DOMContentLoaded would fire
// with `product` still null and lose its content id.

// ── NOTE-18 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Read the CLICKED button: it carries the variant the shopper actually has selected right now,
// and the quantity stepper's value. Off a product page (a quick-add on a listing) there is no
// catalog id to attach — fire the event anyway, without ids. Analytics.astro counts those
// clicks too, and a funnel whose two halves disagree is worse than one event with thin data.

// ── NOTE-19 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `catalogId` is what the PDP stored when the item was added (ADR 0013). A cart written before
// that shipped has none — send the event with no ids rather than the handle, which under the
// number scheme is not a catalog item at all and would quietly match nothing.
// The cart records the id AND the type the PDP resolved for each line, plus qty and price.
// 
// ⚠️ NEVER announce a mixed cart under one type. An earlier version downgraded the whole event
// to `product_group` if any line was group-level, which meant every variant-level id in that
// cart was sent as a group — matching nothing. So the lines are PARTITIONED and the exact ones
// win: a group id only travels when there is nothing more precise in the cart at all (a line
// added before this shipped, or a sparse variant matrix).

// ── NOTE-20 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Purchase — once per order (localStorage guard), with value + content_ids + advanced matching.
// 
// ⚠️ THIS PAGE IS NOT PROOF OF PAYMENT. /checkout/sukses is where an UNPAID virtual account or QR
// is displayed — the shopper still has to go and pay it. Firing Purchase here for a prepaid order
// would report a conversion for every abandoned VA screen and every failed gateway call, and
// inflate the merchant's ROAS with sales that never happened.
// 
// So the SERVER decides, per order, whether this page is a conversion: `tracking.browserPurchase`
// is true for COD (payment on delivery — the order IS the sale, signed off 2026-07-29) and false
// for prepaid, which is counted instead by the Conversions API when the gateway callback
// confirms the money. Same `event_id` on both sides, so nothing is ever counted twice.
// 
// Consequence, stated plainly: a store on prepaid channels with server-side tracking OFF records
// no Purchase at all. That is the honest half of the trade — the Seller UI says so on the card.

// ── NOTE-21 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// POSITIVE gate: fire only when the server SAID this is a conversion. The negative form
// (`=== false`) leaked — if the server's enqueue threw, `tracking` comes back null, the order
// has no flag, and a prepaid sale on an unpaid VA page was reported as revenue anyway.
// Orders written before this shipped carry no flag either, and they are equally unknowable.

// ── NOTE-22 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Ids + de-dup key from the SERVER (order API `tracking`). The cart's handles are the
// fallback for an order placed before this shipped — group-level, so they still match a
// catalog item for single-variant products and the item_group for the rest.

// ── NOTE-23 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Server ids when we have them; otherwise the ids the PDP recorded in the cart. NEVER the
// handle — under the stable scheme (ADR 0013) a handle appears nowhere in the feed, so it
// would report a purchase against an item that does not exist. An old cart with no
// `catalogId` yields an empty list, and no ids is better than wrong ids.

// ── NOTE-24 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Only when we actually HAVE a hash. `crypto.subtle` is undefined on an http:// origin,
// so both come back null there — re-initing the pixel with `{}` would be a pointless
// extra init observed in the browser run of 2026-07-29.
// ⚠️ Meta documents advanced matching at the FIRST `init`; re-initing later is what the
// community reports as unreliable, and no official answer exists. This is best-effort
// for the browser copy — the server event always carries the hashed identity, and that
// is the path we rely on.

// ── NOTE-25 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Google: `user_data` is the **Google Ads** enhanced-conversions contract (an `AW-` tag),
// and gtag hashes it itself — so it takes the RAW values, unlike the two above. It is a
// no-op on a pure GA4 `G-` tag, so we do not hand a shopper's plaintext email and phone
// to a script that has no use for them. Phone must be E.164 WITH the `+`, or Google
// matches nothing.

---

{/* ⚠️ gtag.js is NOT a <head> <script async> any more — it is injected by `later()` in the inline
    script below, together with Meta's and TikTok's. See the DEFERRED LOADING note there for the
    measurement. `async` still blocks: it competes for the main thread the moment it arrives. */}

{pixels.length > 0 && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ META: meta, GOOGLE: google, TIKTOK: tiktok }}>
    (function () {
      // see NOTE-1 in the frontmatter above
      function safe(fn) { try { fn(); } catch (e) {} }

      // see NOTE-2 in the frontmatter above
      // ── DEFERRED VENDOR LOADING ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      // MEASURED, production, Lighthouse mobile, 2026-07-31: switching these three pixels ON took the
      // page from **90 to 66** and Total Blocking Time from **130 ms to 770 ms**. The downloads were
      // never the problem — all three were already `async`. The cost is PARSE + EXECUTE of three
      // third-party bundles on the main thread, during the same window the browser is trying to paint.
      //
      // What is deferred is ONLY the vendor <script>. Every stub, queue and `init` below still runs
      // synchronously, so an `fbq()` / `gtag()` / `ttq.track()` fired anywhere on this page — including
      // by a shopper who converts in the first second — is buffered and replayed IN ORDER the moment
      // the vendor script arrives. That buffering is the vendors' own documented design (`fbq.queue`,
      // `dataLayer`, `ttq`'s method array), not something invented here. NO EVENT IS LOST.
      //
      // ⚠️ FIRST INTERACTION, with a 10 s backstop — deliberately NOT `requestIdleCallback`.
      //
      // Owner decision, 2026-08-01, taken on measured numbers rather than preference. With `rIC` the
      // browser went idle at ~2 s on a fast connection, so all three bundles still executed inside
      // Lighthouse's TBT window: mobile stuck at 75 with TBT 690 ms, of which 600 ms was third-party.
      // Loading on interaction instead takes TBT back to roughly the pixel-free baseline.
      //
      // THE COST, STATED PLAINLY: a visitor who lands and leaves inside 10 s WITHOUT touching the page
      // is never counted as a PageView by Meta/Google/TikTok. That is real bounce data, and it was
      // traded knowingly. What is NOT lost is revenue attribution — a conversion is built from the
      // ORDER ROW on the server (ADR 0012), never from the browser, and a shopper who buys has by
      // definition interacted. `/api/track` (our own funnel analytics) is untouched and still fires on
      // every page view.
      //
      // If this is ever revisited, revisit it with a measurement, not an opinion.
      var fired = false, queued = [];
      function later(fn) {
        queued.push(fn);
        if (fired) return;
        fired = true;
        var go = function () {
          if (!queued.length) return;
          var q = queued; queued = [];
          for (var i = 0; i < q.length; i++) safe(q[i]);
        };
        var evs = ["pointerdown", "keydown", "touchstart", "scroll"];
        var once = function () {
          for (var i = 0; i < evs.length; i++) window.removeEventListener(evs[i], once);
          go();
        };
        for (var i = 0; i < evs.length; i++) window.addEventListener(evs[i], once, { passive: true, once: true });
        setTimeout(once, 10000);
      }
      function inject(src, extra) {
        var e = document.createElement("script");
        e.async = true; e.src = src;
        if (extra) extra(e);
        var s0 = document.getElementsByTagName("script")[0];
        s0.parentNode.insertBefore(e, s0);
      }

      safe(function () {
      if (META) {
        // Stub + queue + init: synchronous, so ordering is preserved. Only the download is deferred.
        var f = window;
        if (!f.fbq) {
          var n = (f.fbq = function () { n.callMethod ? n.callMethod.apply(n, arguments) : n.queue.push(arguments); });
          if (!f._fbq) f._fbq = n; n.push = n; n.loaded = !0; n.version = "2.0"; n.queue = [];
        }
        window.fbq("init", META.pixelId);
        later(function () { inject("https://connect.facebook.net/en_US/fbevents.js"); });
      }
      });
      safe(function () {
      if (GOOGLE) {
        window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
        window.gtag = function () { window.dataLayer.push(arguments); };
        window.gtag("js", new Date());
        window.gtag("config", GOOGLE.pixelId); // auto page_view
        // see NOTE-3 in the frontmatter above
        if (GOOGLE.adsSendTo) window.gtag("config", String(GOOGLE.adsSendTo).split("/")[0]);
        // `dataLayer` already holds js/config//every event; gtag.js drains it in order on arrival.
        later(function () { inject("https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=" + encodeURIComponent(GOOGLE.pixelId)); });
      }
      });
      safe(function () {
      if (TIKTOK) {
        !(function (w, d, t) {
          w.TiktokAnalyticsObject = t; var ttq = (w[t] = w[t] || []);
          ttq.methods = ["page","track","identify","instances","debug","on","off","once","ready","alias","group","enableCookie","disableCookie"];
          ttq.setAndDefer = function (o, m) { o[m] = function () { o.push([m].concat(Array.prototype.slice.call(arguments, 0))); }; };
          for (var i = 0; i < ttq.methods.length; i++) ttq.setAndDefer(ttq, ttq.methods[i]);
          ttq.load = function (id) { var u = "https://analytics.tiktok.com/i18n/pixel/events.js"; ttq._i = ttq._i || {}; ttq._i[id] = []; ttq._i[id]._u = u; ttq._t = ttq._t || {}; ttq._t[id] = +new Date(); ttq._o = ttq._o || {}; ttq._o[id] = {}; var e = d.createElement("script"); e.type = "text/javascript"; e.async = !0; e.src = u + "?sdkid=" + id + "&lib=" + t; var s = d.getElementsByTagName("script")[0]; s.parentNode.insertBefore(e, s); };
          // `ttq.methods` are deferred above, so `ttq.track(...)` anywhere on this page buffers whether
          // or not the SDK has arrived. Only `load` (which is what injects the script) waits.
          ttq.page();
          later(function () { ttq.load(TIKTOK.pixelId); });
        })(window, document, "ttq");
      }
      });

      // see NOTE-4 in the frontmatter above
      // ⚠️ TWO PROVIDERS, TWO FORMATS. Meta wants the digits WITH the country code and WITHOUT a `+`;
      // TikTok and Google want E.164, i.e. the same digits WITH a `+`. `digits()` returns the Meta
      // shape, so hashing it for TikTok produced a digest that matches nothing — and disagreed with
      // our own server, which hashes `+62…` for TikTok (lib/capi.ts). Same trap the server-side file
      // documents; the browser walked into it.
      function e164(s) { var d = digits(s); return d ? "+" + d : ""; }
      function digits(s) {
        s = String(s || "").replace(/[^\d]/g, "");
        if (s.slice(0, 1) === "0") return "62" + s.slice(1);
        if (s.slice(0, 1) === "8") return "62" + s;
        return s;
      }
      async function sha256(s) {
        try {
          var buf = await crypto.subtle.digest("SHA-256", new TextEncoder().encode(String(s)));
          return Array.prototype.map.call(new Uint8Array(buf), function (b) { return b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0"); }).join("");
        } catch (e) { return null; }
      }
      // see NOTE-5 in the frontmatter above
      function ready(fn) {
        if (document.readyState === "loading") document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", fn, { once: true });
        else fn();
      }

      // see NOTE-6 in the frontmatter above
      function fire(evt, data) {
        data = data || {};
        var ids = data.contentIds || [];
        var ctype = data.contentType || "product";
        var qty = data.numItems || 1;
        // see NOTE-7 in the frontmatter above
        var perItemQty = ids.length === 1 ? qty : null;
        var val = typeof data.value === "number" && data.value > 0 ? data.value : null;
        // The GOODS money — price × quantity, no ongkir, no PPN. Only a Purchase can differ from
        // `value`; on every browsing event `value` already IS the goods total, so it stands in.
        var goods = typeof data.goods === "number" && data.goods > 0 ? data.goods : evt !== "Purchase" && val !== null ? val : null;
        var cur = data.currency || "IDR";
        if (META) safe(function () {
          var opts = data.eventId ? { eventID: data.eventId } : undefined;
          if (evt === "PageView") { window.fbq("track", "PageView"); return; }
          // see NOTE-8 in the frontmatter above
          var d = { content_ids: ids, content_type: ctype };
          if (val !== null) { d.value = val; d.currency = cur; }
          if (evt === "InitiateCheckout") d.num_items = qty;
          if (evt === "Purchase") { d = { value: data.value || 0, currency: cur, content_ids: ids, content_type: "product", order_id: data.orderId }; }
          if (opts) window.fbq("track", evt, d, opts);
          else window.fbq("track", evt, d);
        });
        if (TIKTOK) safe(function () {
          // see NOTE-9 in the frontmatter above
          var map = { ViewContent: "ViewContent", AddToCart: "AddToCart", InitiateCheckout: "InitiateCheckout", Purchase: "Purchase" };
          if (evt === "PageView") return; // ttq.page() already fired at load
          var name = map[evt]; if (!name) return;
          // see NOTE-10 in the frontmatter above
          var props = {
            content_type: ctype,
            contents: ids.map(function (id) {
              var c = { content_id: id, content_type: ctype };
              if (perItemQty !== null) c.quantity = perItemQty;
              // TikTok reads `price` as the PER-ITEM price and `value` as the order total. `value` on a
              // Purchase INCLUDES ongkir and PPN, so `value / quantity` was neither the item's price nor
              // anything in the catalog — a 2-unit Rp189.000 order with Rp100.000 shipping published
              // `price: 239.000`, disagreeing with the same SKU in the product feed. Use the goods
              // subtotal when we have one, and send no price at all rather than an invented one.
              if (perItemQty !== null && goods !== null) c.price = Math.round(goods / perItemQty);
              return c;
            }),
            quantity: qty,
            currency: cur,
          };
          // see NOTE-11 in the frontmatter above
          if (ids.length === 1) props.content_id = ids[0];
          if (val !== null) props.value = val;
          else if (evt === "Purchase") props.value = data.value || 0;
          // see NOTE-12 in the frontmatter above
          if (data.eventId) window.ttq.track(name, props, { event_id: data.eventId });
          else window.ttq.track(name, props);
        });
        if (GOOGLE) safe(function () {
          var map = { ViewContent: "view_item", AddToCart: "add_to_cart", InitiateCheckout: "begin_checkout", Purchase: "purchase" };
          if (evt === "PageView") return; // config already sent page_view
          var name = map[evt]; if (!name) return;
          // see NOTE-13 in the frontmatter above
          // ⚠️ `send_to` IS REQUIRED HERE. Without it gtag broadcasts the event to the DEFAULT group —
          // every destination `config`'d on the page, which includes the AW- tag below. One purchase
          // then reached Google Ads twice (once as this event, once as the remarketing copy), and the
          // two are not dedupable because only this one carries `transaction_id`.
          var payload = { send_to: GOOGLE.pixelId, currency: cur, items: (data.lineItems && data.lineItems.length ? data.lineItems : ids.map(function (id) { return { id: id }; })).map(function (li) {
            var it = { item_id: li.id };
            if (typeof li.price === "number") it.price = li.price;
            if (typeof li.qty === "number") it.quantity = li.qty;
            else if (perItemQty !== null) it.quantity = perItemQty;
            return it;
          }) };
          // GA4 defines purchase `value` as the sum of price × quantity, explicitly WITHOUT shipping and
          // tax, and gives both their own parameters. We were sending the order total, so GA4 revenue
          // was overstated by ongkir + PPN on every order and could never be reconciled against the
          // seller's own Pesanan screen. Meta and TikTok have no such rule — they still get the total.
          var ga4Value = goods !== null ? goods : evt === "Purchase" ? data.value || 0 : null;
          if (ga4Value !== null) payload.value = ga4Value;
          if (typeof data.shipping === "number" && data.shipping > 0) payload.shipping = data.shipping;
          if (typeof data.tax === "number" && data.tax > 0) payload.tax = data.tax;
          if (evt === "Purchase" && data.orderId) payload.transaction_id = data.orderId;
          window.gtag("event", name, payload);

          // see NOTE-14 in the frontmatter above
          if (!GOOGLE.adsSendTo) return;
          var awId = String(GOOGLE.adsSendTo).split("/")[0];
          var rmk = {
            send_to: awId,
            value: payload.value,
            currency: cur,
            items: ids.map(function (id) { return { id: id, google_business_vertical: "retail" }; }),
          };
          // Remarketing on every funnel step; the conversion only where money changes hands.
          // `add_to_cart` is one of Google's recognised retail remarketing events, and "added to cart,
          // did not buy" is the highest-intent audience a shop has — collapsing it into `view_item`
          // threw that segmentation away. `begin_checkout` is NOT in the retail list, so it stays
          // `view_item`.
          window.gtag("event", name === "purchase" ? "purchase" : name === "add_to_cart" ? "add_to_cart" : "view_item", rmk);
          if (evt === "Purchase") {
            window.gtag("event", "conversion", {
              send_to: GOOGLE.adsSendTo,
              value: data.value || 0,
              currency: cur,
              transaction_id: data.orderId,
            });
          }
        });
      }

      // see NOTE-15 in the frontmatter above
      safe(function () {
        var m = location.search.match(/[?&]ttclid=([^&#]+)/);
        if (!m) return;
        var v = decodeURIComponent(m[1]).slice(0, 256);
        try { localStorage.setItem("tphi:ttclid", JSON.stringify({ v: v, t: Date.now() })); } catch (e) {}
      });

      // UTM, captured the same way and for the same reason: it is in the URL of the landing page ONCE,
      // and by checkout it is gone. It matters more than any cookie here — `utm_medium` is the ONLY
      // signal that separates a paid Meta click from an organic one. Meta appends `fbclid` to every
      // outbound link it serves, ads or not (Google's `gclid` and TikTok's `ttclid` really are
      // paid-only), so without this a shopper tapping the shop's own Instagram bio link would be booked
      // as ad revenue. 30 days, matching the attribution window on the server.
      safe(function () {
        var q = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
        var src = (q.get("utm_source") || "").slice(0, 64);
        var med = (q.get("utm_medium") || "").slice(0, 64);
        if (!src && !med) return;
        try { localStorage.setItem("tphi:utm", JSON.stringify({ s: src, m: med, t: Date.now() })); } catch (e) {}
      });

      var path = location.pathname.replace(/\/+$/, "") || "/";

      // PageView on every page (fbq/ttq already sent theirs at init; this is the funnel entry point).
      fire("PageView", {});

      // see NOTE-16 in the frontmatter above
      function ctxFrom(el) {
        if (!el) return null;
        var id = el.getAttribute("data-catalog-id");
        if (!id) return null;
        var price = Number(el.getAttribute("data-price") || 0);
        return {
          contentIds: [id],
          contentType: el.getAttribute("data-catalog-type") || "product",
          value: price > 0 ? price : undefined,
        };
      }
      function productContext() {
        if (path.indexOf("/produk/") !== 0) return null;
        return ctxFrom(document.getElementById("add-to-cart")) || ctxFrom(document.querySelector("[data-catalog-id]"));
      }
      // Resolved AFTER the DOM exists — see ready(). Everything downstream reads this variable, so it
      // is assigned there and the events that depend on it fire from inside that callback.
      var product = null;
      ready(function () {
        product = productContext();
        // ViewContent — product detail
        if (product) fire("ViewContent", product);

        // see NOTE-17 in the frontmatter above
        document.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
          var t = e.target; if (!t || !t.closest) return;
          var btn = t.closest("#add-to-cart, #buy-now, [data-track='add_to_cart']"); if (!btn || btn.disabled) return;
          // see NOTE-18 in the frontmatter above
          var ctx = ctxFrom(btn) || product || {};
          var q = Number(btn.getAttribute("data-qty") || 0);
          fire("AddToCart", q > 0 ? Object.assign({}, ctx, { numItems: q, value: ctx.value ? ctx.value * q : undefined }) : ctx);
        }, true);
      });

      // InitiateCheckout — checkout page opened
      if (path === "/checkout") {
        // see NOTE-19 in the frontmatter above
        var cart = [];
        try { cart = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("cart") || "[]") || []; } catch (e) {}
        // ⚠️ THE MONEY MUST DESCRIBE THE SAME LINES AS THE IDS. This used to partition the cart into
        // exact vs group ids and let the exact ones win — while summing `units` and `total` over ALL
        // lines. So a cart of "Rose/L ×2 @189.000" plus one group-level line ×1 @250.000 reported one
        // content id with num_items 3 and value 628.000: three units of a product the shopper has two
        // of, at a price that includes an item never named.
        var exact = [], groups = [], exactUnits = 0, exactTotal = 0, groupUnits = 0, groupTotal = 0;
        cart.forEach(function (i) {
          if (!i || !i.catalogId) return;
          var q = Number(i.qty) || 1;
          var line = (Number(i.price) || 0) * q;
          if (i.catalogType === "product_group") { groups.push(i.catalogId); groupUnits += q; groupTotal += line; }
          else { exact.push(i.catalogId); exactUnits += q; exactTotal += line; }
        });
        var useExact = exact.length > 0;
        var icUnits = useExact ? exactUnits : groupUnits;
        var icTotal = useExact ? exactTotal : groupTotal;
        fire("InitiateCheckout", {
          contentIds: useExact ? exact : groups,
          contentType: useExact ? "product" : "product_group",
          // Real numbers, not 1 and 0: `num_items` is the field Meta documents for this event, and a
          // begin_checkout with no value tells GA4 and TikTok nothing about what is at stake.
          numItems: icUnits || 1,
          value: icTotal > 0 ? icTotal : undefined,
          goods: icTotal > 0 ? icTotal : undefined,
        });
      }
      // see NOTE-20 in the frontmatter above
      // ⚠️ BOTH conversion pages. `/checkout/sukses` is the storefront checkout's; `/thanks` is the ad
      // funnel's (/promo, /lp). The funnel's page was omitted, so the one flow a merchant PAYS for
      // recorded no browser Purchase at all — and the `browserPurchase` gate below is what keeps that
      // honest either way, because the server decides per order whether the money has actually landed.
      if (path === "/checkout/sukses" || path === "/thanks") {
        var order = null;
        try { order = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("lastOrder") || "null"); } catch (e) {}
        // see NOTE-21 in the frontmatter above
        if (order && !(order.tracking && order.tracking.browserPurchase === true)) order = null;
        if (order) {
          var key = String(order.number || order.createdAt || "");
          var seen = [];
          try { seen = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("tphi:px:purchased") || "[]"); } catch (e) {}
          if (!Array.isArray(seen)) seen = [];
          if (key && seen.indexOf(key) === -1) {
            seen.push(key);
            try { localStorage.setItem("tphi:px:purchased", JSON.stringify(seen.slice(-20))); } catch (e) {}
            var items = Array.isArray(order.items) ? order.items : [];
            // see NOTE-22 in the frontmatter above
            var tr = order.tracking || {};
            // see NOTE-23 in the frontmatter above
            var ids = Array.isArray(tr.contentIds) && tr.contentIds.length
              ? tr.contentIds
              : items.map(function (i) { return i && i.catalogId; }).filter(Boolean);
            var qty = items.reduce(function (n, i) { return n + (i && i.qty ? Number(i.qty) : 1); }, 0);
            // The success page holds what the SERVER priced (checkout.astro overwrites subtotal, tax,
            // shipping and the line prices from the order response), so GA4 can be given its documented
            // shape — value excluding ongkir and PPN, with both as their own parameters — instead of the
            // order total. Per-line prices only when the ids and the lines line up one-for-one; a
            // mismatch means a line had no resolvable catalog id, and half-mapped items are worse than
            // none.
            // subtotal MINUS discount — byte-identical to the server's `facts.goods`
            // (lib/tracking-events.ts). `res.subtotal` is the PRE-discount goods total and `res.discount`
            // was returned and never read, so a voucher made the gtag purchase and the Measurement
            // Protocol purchase report two different values for one `transaction_id`.
            var goods = Math.max(0, Number(order.subtotal || 0) - Number(order.discount || 0)) || null;
            var shipCost = Number(order.shipping && order.shipping.price || 0) || null;
            var taxAmt = Number(order.tax || 0) || null;
            var lineItems = ids.length === items.length
              ? ids.map(function (id, n) {
                  var it = items[n] || {};
                  var li = { id: id };
                  if (Number(it.price) > 0) li.price = Number(it.price);
                  if (Number(it.qty) > 0) li.qty = Number(it.qty);
                  return li;
                })
              : null;
            var data = { value: Number(order.total || 0), currency: "IDR", contentIds: ids, numItems: qty || items.length, orderId: key, eventId: tr.eventId || null,
              goods: goods, shipping: shipCost, tax: taxAmt, lineItems: lineItems };

            // Advanced matching (opt-in per channel): hash email/phone, attach, THEN fire Purchase.
            var email = order.contact && order.contact.email ? String(order.contact.email).trim().toLowerCase() : "";
            var phone = order.shippingAddress && order.shippingAddress.whatsapp ? digits(order.shippingAddress.whatsapp) : "";
            var phoneE164 = order.shippingAddress && order.shippingAddress.whatsapp ? e164(order.shippingAddress.whatsapp) : "";
            var wantAM = (META && META.advancedMatching) || (TIKTOK && TIKTOK.advancedMatching) || (GOOGLE && GOOGLE.advancedMatching);
            if (wantAM && (email || phone)) {
              // THREE hashes, not two: Meta's phone and TikTok's differ by one character, and one
              // digest cannot serve both. Google takes the raw values (gtag hashes them itself).
              Promise.all([email ? sha256(email) : null, phone ? sha256(phone) : null, phoneE164 ? sha256(phoneE164) : null]).then(function (h) {
                var em = h[0], ph = h[1], phPlus = h[2];
                // see NOTE-24 in the frontmatter above
                if (META && META.advancedMatching && (em || ph)) safe(function () { var am = {}; if (em) am.em = em; if (ph) am.ph = ph; window.fbq("init", META.pixelId, am); });
                if (TIKTOK && TIKTOK.advancedMatching && (em || phPlus)) safe(function () { var id = {}; if (em) id.email = em; if (phPlus) id.phone_number = phPlus; window.ttq.identify(id); });
                // see NOTE-25 in the frontmatter above
                // Gated on the ADS destination, not on the pixel field. The Seller UI tells the merchant
                // to put GA4's `G-…` in "ID Pixel/Tag" and the `AW-…/label` in the separate Ads field —
                // so testing `pixelId.slice(0,3) === "AW-"` meant enhanced conversions were silently off
                // for every merchant who wired it the way we documented.
                // `adsSendTo` OR a legacy `AW-` in the pixel field. Gating on `adsSendTo` alone fixed the
                // documented wiring and silently broke the undocumented one that used to work.
                if (GOOGLE && GOOGLE.advancedMatching && (GOOGLE.adsSendTo || String(GOOGLE.pixelId).slice(0, 3) === "AW-")) safe(function () {
                  var ud = {}; if (email) ud.email = email; if (phoneE164) ud.phone_number = phoneE164;
                  window.gtag("set", "user_data", ud);
                });
                fire("Purchase", data);
              }, function () { fire("Purchase", data); });
            } else {
              fire("Purchase", data);
            }
          }
        }
      }
    })();
  </script>
)}
